                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "73090920",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198001454789",
                            "num_reviews": 286,
                            "playtime_forever": 31,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 31,
                            "last_played": 1595384847
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Hotline Miami, but you can take control of enemies. Sounds cool in theory, is tedious as hell. The levels are built in a way such that you have to die. A lot.\n\nYou enter a room and with 5-10 enemies. So you possess an enemy, try to kill as many as possible, and die You possess the next enemy, try to kill as many as possible, and die. Repeat until the room is clear, proceed to the next room, repeat. Enemies won't know you're controlling one of them until you fire a bullet, but then suddenly all enemies in the level will immediately shoot at you. There's no flow, there's no rewarding feeling, it's just a series of trial & error rooms that feel like a chore.\n\nYou can't move the view by pressing Shift like in Hotline Miami, so you will keep running into enemies. Punching them knocks them out silently and allows you to pick up their guns, but you can't kill them until they wake up again and run for a gun.\n\nThe guns don't feel good and empty guns don't despawn, so you'll have to run over all weapons on the ground until you find a non-empty one you can pick up. Guns are always empty in like 2 seconds, so this is a huge issue and it makes the boss fights incredibly frustrating.\n\nOnly 3.8% of all players got the achievement for finishing the first level. That's consistent with my first impression and in level 6 I finally gave my hopes that the game will become fun.",
